From d5db1ec1f932c11695a1f69c9e306a3cf568ba7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:18:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 091/119] scene-referred S7: thresholds in stops above mid
 grey, and Streak Amount back to physical

Every highlight-extraction threshold the camera drives is compared against the HDR target, and
after S1 that target is EXPOSED - a correctly exposed 18 % grey card sits at 0.18 by construction.
An absolute threshold therefore no longer describes a fixed brightness in the world; it describes a
brightness relative to whatever exposure happens to be in force, and it changes meaning every time
the aperture, the shutter, the ISO or the ND moves. That is the "pops in at f/6.4" class of bug,
and it is the one the S7 acceptance test looks for.

Under the switch these parameters are read as STOPS ABOVE MID GREY:

    threshold = 0.18 * 2^clamp(stored, -6, +16)

Off the switch ResolveThreshold is the identity and not one bit of the stock path moves. It is one
function, called at the six places where a stored parameter becomes a shader constant - the DOF
sprite-bokeh highlight threshold, the ghost threshold, halation, the anamorphic flare, the front
filter's star threshold and the streak/diffraction source - so seven effects cannot each grow their
own slightly different idea of what a threshold means. Nothing is serialised differently, no
default moves and no CRC changes; what changes is what a given number MEANS while the switch is on.

The clamp is on the STOPS and not on the linear result, because the ghost and front-filter sliders
have a 1..64 travel that was chosen for absolute values, and 2^64 is not a number any frame
contains. Each site's soft knee is still derived from the resolved threshold, so the fade-in stays
the same width in stops.

Streak Amount is the same story from the other end. The default of 50 was a compensation for lights
that arrived clamped and unexposed: at 1 the wave model deposits the pupil's actual diffracted
energy, and that is invisible when the source has already had its real brightness thrown away. With
the pre-exposure in place the sources are real, so the effective amount is the stored value over
50 - the shipped 50 IS the physical 1.0, saved levels open looking right, the slider still spans
0 to 4x physical, and the default stays where it is.

Two corrections to the spec's S7 wording, recorded there: there is no bloom threshold in CE 5.7.1
to convert (CBloomStage::Execute is a pure separable Gaussian over the whole quarter-res target,
with no high pass anywhere), so bloom's exposure response is entirely S1's pre-exposure; and the
firefly filter is untouched for the same reason.

Tooltips and the README say the unit changes and what the shipped default now means. The mapping
both ways - including log2(T/0.18) for converting a scene that was tuned absolute - is
docs/SceneReferredContent.md section 6.

